    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Hardwood flooring is more than just a surface to walk on; it is a significant investment and a core part of a home&rsquo;s character. Area rugs are the perfect accompaniment to wood, adding warmth, colour and texture, but paired with the wrong rug pad (in the UK usually called a rug underlay) they can work against the floor. Without a barrier, rugs can slide and rub, dulling the finish through friction, and some rug backings and pad materials can mark or discolour a floor&rsquo;s finish over time.</p>
<p>Choosing the right rug pad takes a little thought. It means understanding how the pad&rsquo;s material may interact with your floor&rsquo;s finish, how much foot traffic the area gets, and what the rug itself needs. This guide explores the most effective rug pad types for hardwood floors so your home stays both beautiful and protected. Always check your floor manufacturer&rsquo;s guidance too, as some name specific pad materials to use or avoid.</p>

<h2 id="natural-rubber">1. Natural rubber</h2>
<p>Natural rubber is one of the most popular choices for non-slip rug pads on hardwood. Unlike synthetic rubber or PVC, it is derived from the sap of rubber trees, which makes it a renewable material. Its main advantage is that it grips the floor rather than sticking to it.</p>
<p>Cheaper synthetic pads sometimes rely on adhesives or plasticisers to keep rugs in place. Over time, these can react with some floor finishes, leaving marks, discolouration or a sticky residue that is difficult to remove. Natural rubber is generally considered safe for most sealed hardwood finishes, but check your floor manufacturer&rsquo;s guidance, particularly for oiled or newly finished floors. It provides a strong grip that helps stop rugs bunching or sliding, which matters for safety in busy areas such as hallways and entrances. Good-quality natural rubber is also durable and holds its shape well, giving a consistent protective layer.</p>
<p>One caution: natural rubber is derived from latex, so anyone in the household with a latex allergy should check the product details before buying.</p>

<h2 id="recycled-felt">2. Recycled felt</h2>
<p>For larger rugs anchored by furniture, such as those in dining rooms or bedrooms, grip is often less of a priority than cushioning and protection. This is where recycled felt rug pads excel. They are typically made from recycled carpet fibres and yarn, needle-punched into a dense, thick mat.</p>
<p>Felt pads dampen footstep noise well, making them a good choice for flats and upstairs rooms. Because they are dense rather than squashy, they give a firm foundation that stops the rug&rsquo;s backing rubbing against the wood. That density also helps extend the life of the rug by cushioning footsteps, so the fibres are not crushed against the hard floor. When choosing a felt pad, check how the fibres are bonded and whether the maker says it is suitable for wooden floors. On its own, felt offers little grip, so a rug on an open floor can still slide or ruck up.</p>

<h2 id="rubber-and-felt-hybrid">3. Rubber and felt hybrid</h2>
<p>For most living rooms on hardwood, a hybrid pad is the best all-round option. It combines the grip of natural rubber with the cushioning of felt, usually as a felt top bonded to a natural rubber base.</p>
<p>The rubber side faces the floor and keeps the pad in place without adhesives, while the felt top grabs the back of the rug to stop it shifting on the pad. Lay it rubber side down and felt side up, unless the maker says otherwise. This dual action suits medium-sized rugs in living rooms and under coffee tables. It is thick enough to feel comfortable underfoot while staying low enough not to create a trip edge, and it helps stop thinner rugs from wrinkling.</p>
<p>Whichever pad you choose, cut it slightly smaller than the rug, about 2.5 cm (1 in) in from the edge on each side, so it stays hidden and the rug edges lie flat against the floor. A 200 &times; 300 cm rug, for example, needs a pad of about 195 &times; 295 cm.</p>

<h2 id="memory-foam">4. Memory foam</h2>
<p>When comfort is the priority, memory foam rug pads are worth considering. They are often used under thinner rugs or where people stand for long periods, such as in front of a kitchen sink, or in a child&rsquo;s playroom.</p>
<p>Memory foam gives a level of softness that neither felt nor rubber can match. However, check that the pad specifically states it is suitable for hardwood, and avoid pads with non-breathable backings that can trap moisture and heat against the floor and may affect the finish. Memory foam is usually best kept to lower-traffic spots and paired with a grippy base layer.</p>

<h2 id="low-profile-rubber">5. Low-profile rubber</h2>
<p>Not every rug needs a thick cushion. For flat-weave rugs such as kilims and dhurries, or rugs near doors with little clearance, a low-profile natural rubber pad is the answer. These pads can be as thin as around 1.5 mm (1/16 in) yet still give good grip to keep the rug in place.</p>
<p>Low-profile pads are designed to be invisible. They provide the friction that stops a rug sliding when a door opens over it or someone rounds a corner quickly. Despite their thinness, they still form a protective layer that stops the coarse underside of a woven rug acting like sandpaper on the floor&rsquo;s finish. Before buying any pad, measure the gap under doors that will swing over the rug and make sure the combined height of rug and pad clears it comfortably.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Lay the pad first, then unroll the rug onto it from one end rather than dropping the rug on top. It is much easier to keep everything square, and you will not have to drag the rug across the pad.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="materials-to-avoid">Materials to avoid</h2>
<p>When shopping for rug pads for hardwood, be cautious with these materials:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>PVC (polyvinyl chloride):</strong> often used in inexpensive mesh pads sold on a roll. Some PVC pads contain plasticisers that can react with certain floor finishes over time, leaving a pattern, discolouration or a cloudy or sticky residue, particularly in warm rooms or on underfloor heating. If you do use one, check that both the pad maker and your floor manufacturer say it is suitable for your finish.</li>
<li><strong>Adhesive backings:</strong> a pad that feels sticky to the touch may use a glue-like coating that can transfer to your floor, and the residue may need professional attention to remove.</li>
<li><strong>Synthetic latex with fillers:</strong> some synthetic latex pads contain fillers that can harden and crumble into a fine, gritty dust over time.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="choosing-by-traffic-and-rug-type">Choosing based on traffic and rug type</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>High traffic (hallways, entrances):</strong> use a natural rubber or hybrid pad. Safety comes first here, so choose a pad with good, reliable grip to help prevent slips.</li>
<li><strong>Comfort areas (living rooms, bedrooms):</strong> use a felt and rubber hybrid, or memory foam where it suits. The extra thickness feels more luxurious and helps dampen sound.</li>
<li><strong>Thin and flat-weave rugs:</strong> use a low-profile rubber pad to keep the look of the rug without adding bulk.</li>
<li><strong>Large, heavy rugs:</strong> use recycled felt. The weight of the rug and furniture will usually keep it in place, so the focus is on floor protection and sound dampening.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Safety first:</strong> choose a pad that stops the rug sliding, to help avoid accidents and friction wear on the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Finish compatibility:</strong> natural rubber and felt are the safer choices for most wooden floors. Be wary of PVC and adhesive-backed pads, and check your floor manufacturer&rsquo;s guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Breathability:</strong> avoid pads that can trap moisture against the floor; needle-punched felt and breathable rubber are good options.</li>
<li><strong>Protecting your investment:</strong> a high-quality rug pad costs a fraction of a floor refinishing job, so it is not the place to cut corners.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="rug-without-a-pad">Can I use a rug without a pad if the rug is heavy?</h3>
<p>Even if a rug is heavy enough not to slide, a pad is still recommended. The backing of many rugs, especially those with latex or stiff coatings, can be abrasive. Over time, the small movements of people walking on the rug can make it act like sandpaper, dulling the finish of your floor.</p>
<h3 id="how-often-to-replace">How often should I replace my rug pad?</h3>
<p>A good-quality natural rubber or felt pad can last many years with care. Check it every so often, though: if the rubber feels brittle or starts to crumble, or the felt has become flattened and lost its cushioning, it is time for a replacement.</p>
<h3 id="sun-fading">Will a rug pad prevent my floor from fading in the sun?</h3>
<p>A rug pad will not stop UV light, and wood naturally changes colour with sunlight. To avoid uneven fading, where the wood under the rug stays darker while the rest of the floor lightens, move or rotate your rugs occasionally and consider UV-filtering window treatments.</p>
<h3 id="underfloor-heating">Are rug pads safe for underfloor heating?</h3>
<p>Not all of them. Radiant heated floors, usually called underfloor heating in the UK, work best with thin floor coverings, because every layer you add insulates the floor and slows heat getting into the room. Heating manufacturers often give a maximum combined tog value (thermal resistance) for rugs and pads; a combined figure of around 2.5 tog is commonly quoted in the UK, but follow the guidance for your own system. Choose a thin natural rubber or felt and rubber pad labelled as suitable for underfloor heating, avoid PVC pads unless the maker explicitly approves them, and check your heating system&rsquo;s and pad&rsquo;s guidance. If in doubt, ask your heating installer.</p>
<h3 id="cleaning-a-rug-pad">How do I clean a rug pad?</h3>
<p>Most felt and rubber pads can be vacuumed with a floor attachment rather than a beater bar. For spills, blot the pad with a damp cloth and a little mild detergent. Always make sure the pad and floor are completely dry before relaying the rug, so moisture is not trapped against the wood. Every few months, lift the rug and pad and vacuum the floor underneath, as trapped grit is what scratches the finish.</p>

